\title{A Symmetry Dichotomy for Attention Architectures:\\
Exact Expressive Power, Sharp Barriers, and the Role of Positional Encoding}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We determine the exact approximation-theoretic reach of attention-based sequence models
constrained by a group of token symmetries, and we quantify precisely what that constraint
costs. Working with sequences $x \in \R^{\iota\times\kappa}$ over finite index sets $\iota$
of positions and $\kappa$ of features, and with an arbitrary finite group $\Gamma$ acting on
positions through a homomorphism $\act : \Gamma \to \Sym(\iota)$, we prove a two-sided
dichotomy for the \emph{attention algebra} $\A$: the unital $\R$-subalgebra of
$C(\R^{\iota\times\kappa},\R)$ generated by linear attention readouts, equivalently by softmax
attention heads with uniform scores. On the positive side, on every compact
$\Gamma$-saturated domain every $\Gamma$-invariant continuous functional is a uniform limit of
$\Gamma$-invariant elements of $\A$; the proof combines the Stone--Weierstrass theorem with
group averaging, which is legitimate because $\A$ is stable under relabelling of positions.
On the negative side, the $\Gamma$-invariant class is uniformly closed, and on each orbit the
optimal invariant error equals \emph{exactly} half the orbit oscillation of the target,
attained by the constant orbit-midpoint model; consequently no invariant model reads an
absolute position to uniform accuracy better than $1/2$. With the one-hot positional
encoding, fully permutation-invariant models regain complete universality --- for scalar
functionals and for sequence-to-sequence maps --- via an explicit permutation-invariant
continuous decoder that inverts the encoding, so positional encoding works not by breaking
the model's symmetry but by relocating order information into the data. Finally, through a
marked-token encoding, continuous $\Gamma$-equivariant sequence-to-sequence maps are
\emph{precisely} the marked evaluations of continuous $\Gamma$-invariant functionals; symmetric
attention therefore uniformly approximates every continuous equivariant map, and the
constructed head family is exactly, not approximately, equivariant.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Attention-based sequence models are, in raw form, indifferent to order. A transformer layer
without positional information treats its input as a multiset of tokens: permuting the input
permutes the output and nothing else changes. Practitioners repair this by adding positional
encodings, and the repair is universally regarded as necessary. But ``necessary'' is a
qualitative statement. Three quantitative questions have remained open.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \emph{Exactly which} functionals of a sequence can a symmetry-constrained attention model
approximate?
\item \emph{How badly} does it fail on the rest --- is there a formula for the optimal error, or
only a nonzero lower bound?
\item \emph{How completely} does positional encoding repair the deficit, and by what mechanism?
\end{enumerate}

We answer all three, at the natural level of generality: not just full permutation symmetry,
but an arbitrary finite group of token symmetries. The extra generality is not decorative.
Relative positional encodings impose \emph{cyclic} rather than full symmetry; hierarchical or
block-structured attention imposes wreath-product symmetry; set-of-sets architectures impose
products of symmetric groups. All are instances of one theorem.

\subsection{The shape of the answer}

For a finite group $\Gamma$ acting on positions, let $\A^{\Gamma}$ denote the $\Gamma$-invariant
part of the attention algebra. The main results say
\[
  \overline{\A^{\Gamma}}\big|_K \;=\; \bigl\{\, g \in C(K,\R) \;:\; g \text{ is }
  \Gamma\text{-invariant} \,\bigr\}
\]
for every compact $\Gamma$-saturated $K$, closure in the uniform norm. The inclusion
$\subseteq$ is a uniform-closedness statement, the inclusion $\supseteq$ a density statement.
Off the invariant class, the distance from a target $g$ to $\A^{\Gamma}$, measured on a single
orbit, is exactly half the orbit oscillation. Three further theorems complete the picture: a
representation theorem identifying equivariant maps with marked invariant functionals; an
equivariant universality theorem; and the positional-restoration theorems.

\subsection{Relation to classical universality}

Classical universal-approximation results establish density of an unconstrained model class in
$C(K,\R)$. They are one-sided: they say what is achievable and are silent on what is not. The
content of the present work is that when a symmetry constraint is imposed, the two-sided answer
is available in closed form, and the barrier is computable rather than merely nonzero. The
technique for the positive half --- averaging a Stone--Weierstrass approximant over the group
--- is elementary; its correctness hinges on a stability lemma for the attention algebra, which
is where the specific structure of attention enters.

\section{Setup and definitions}

Throughout, $\iota$ and $\kappa$ are finite nonempty index sets; write $n = |\iota|$ for the
sequence length and $d = |\kappa|$ for the feature dimension.

\begin{definition}[Sequences]
A \emph{sequence} is an element of $\Seq(\iota,\kappa) = (\iota \to \kappa \to \R) \cong
\R^{n\times d}$, i.e.\ an $n\times d$ real table $x$ with $x_{i,a}$ the $a$-th feature of the
$i$-th token, endowed with the product topology.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Token action]
For $\sigma \in \Sym(\iota)$ set $(\sigma\cdot x)_i = x_{\sigma(i)}$. This satisfies
$\sigma\cdot(\tau\cdot x) = (\tau\sigma)\cdot x$, fixes the identity permutation, and each
$\sigma\cdot(-)$ is a coordinate permutation, hence a continuous linear isometry.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Symmetry group]
A \emph{token-symmetry group} is a pair $(\Gamma,\act)$ with $\Gamma$ finite and
$\act : \Gamma\to\Sym(\iota)$ a homomorphism. We abbreviate $g\cdot x := \act(g)\cdot x$. Two
running specializations: $\Gamma = \Sym(\iota)$ with $\act = \mathrm{id}$ (\emph{full permutation
symmetry}, the symmetry of an attention stack with no positional information), and
$\Gamma = \langle r\rangle$ cyclic (\emph{shift symmetry}, the symmetry of relative positional
encoding).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Invariance, saturation]
A functional $f$ is \emph{$\Gamma$-invariant} if $f(g\cdot x)=f(x)$ for all $g\in\Gamma$ and all
$x$. A set $K$ is \emph{$\Gamma$-saturated} if $x\in K$ implies $g\cdot x\in K$ for all
$g\in\Gamma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Readouts]
For a weight table $w\in\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ the \emph{readout} $R_w$ is
$R_w(x) = \sum_{i}\sum_{a} w_{i,a}x_{i,a}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Softmax attention head]
For scores $s:\iota\to\R$ and value weights $w$,
\[
  H_{s,w}(x) \;=\; \sum_{j\in\iota} \frac{e^{s_j}}{\sum_{k\in\iota} e^{s_k}}
  \Bigl( \sum_{a\in\kappa} w_{j,a}\,x_{j,a} \Bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Readouts are attention heads]\label{prop:head}
For all $w$ and $x$, $H_{0,w}(x) = R_{w/n}(x)$, where $(w/n)_{j,a} = w_{j,a}/n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
With $s\equiv 0$ every exponential equals $1$, so each softmax weight is $1/n$. Distributing
the constant $1/n$ through the inner sum gives $\sum_j\sum_a (w_{j,a}/n)x_{j,a}$.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:head} licenses the next definition as a model of \emph{attention}: the
generators are literally attention heads, in the uniform-score regime.

\begin{definition}[Attention algebra]
The \emph{attention algebra} $\A = \A(\iota,\kappa)$ is the unital $\R$-subalgebra of
$C(\Seq(\iota,\kappa),\R)$ generated by $\{R_w : w\in\Seq(\iota,\kappa)\}$; equivalently, the
set of real polynomials in finitely many readouts.
\end{definition}

Two remarks justify $\A$ as a proxy for a deep attention stack. Sums of readouts model
multi-head attention with a linear output projection; products model the multiplicative
interactions generated by stacking layers with gating and residual connections, since a
depth-$L$ stack computes, in its polynomial part, products of layerwise linear reads. We
therefore study the closure of the architecture class under exactly the operations the
architecture implements.

\begin{definition}[One-hot positional encoding]
$\enc : \Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\iota)$ is
$\enc(x)_i = \bigl(x_i,\ \one_{\{\cdot = i\}}\bigr)$: token $i$ keeps its $\kappa$-block and
acquires an $\iota$-block whose $j$-th coordinate is $1$ if $j=i$ and $0$ otherwise. It is
continuous, each coordinate being a projection or a constant.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Marked-token encoding]
For $i\in\iota$, $m_i : \Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\{\ast\})$ is
$m_i(x)_j = \bigl(x_j,\ \one_{\{j=i\}}\bigr)$. For a marked sequence $z$ write
$\feat(z)_{i,a} = z_{i,a}$ and $\mrk(z)_i = z_{i,\ast}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equivariance]
$F : \Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ is \emph{$\Gamma$-equivariant} if
$F(g\cdot x)_{i,a} = F(x)_{\act(g)(i),a}$ for all $g,x,i,a$.
\end{definition}

\section{Stability and separation}

\begin{lemma}[Readouts separate sequences]\label{lem:sep}
If $x\neq y$ then some readout takes different values at $x$ and $y$. Consequently $\A$
separates points.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Pick $i_0,a_0$ with $x_{i_0,a_0}\neq y_{i_0,a_0}$ and let $w$ be the one-hot table
$w_{i,a} = \one_{\{i=i_0,\,a=a_0\}}$. Then $R_w(z) = z_{i_0,a_0}$ for every $z$, so
$R_w(x)\neq R_w(y)$.
\end{proof}

Note the by-product: every coordinate evaluation $z\mapsto z_{i_0,a_0}$ is a readout, hence a
uniform-score attention head. This is used repeatedly.

\begin{lemma}[Stability under the token action]\label{lem:stab}
For every $\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)$, precomposition with $\sigma\cdot(-)$ maps $\A$ into $\A$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Precomposition with a fixed continuous map is a unital $\R$-algebra homomorphism, so it
suffices to check generators. Reindexing the outer sum by $\sigma$,
\[
  R_w(\sigma\cdot x) = \sum_i\sum_a w_{i,a}x_{\sigma(i),a}
  = \sum_i\sum_a w_{\sigma^{-1}(i),a}x_{i,a} = R_{w\circ\sigma^{-1}}(x),
\]
so the image of a readout is a readout. The preimage of $\A$ under an algebra map is a
subalgebra containing all generators, hence contains $\A$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} is where the architecture's structure matters: a model class not closed
under relabelling positions cannot be symmetrized, and the entire positive half collapses.

\begin{definition}[Symmetrization]
$(\Sy_\Gamma q)(x) = \frac{1}{|\Gamma|}\sum_{g\in\Gamma} q(g\cdot x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Properties of symmetrization]\label{lem:sym}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\Sy_\Gamma q$ is $\Gamma$-invariant.
\item If $q\in\A$ then $\Sy_\Gamma q\in\A$.
\item \emph{(Averaging is free.)} If $f$ is $\Gamma$-invariant, $K$ is $\Gamma$-saturated, and
$|q-f|<\varepsilon$ on $K$, then $|\Sy_\Gamma q - f| < \varepsilon$ on $K$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(i) For $\tau\in\Gamma$, reindex by $g\mapsto\tau g$ and use $\tau\cdot(g\cdot x) = (g\tau)\cdot
x$. (ii) Each summand lies in $\A$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:stab}, and subalgebras are closed under
finite sums and scalar multiples. (iii) Fix $x\in K$; saturation gives $g\cdot x\in K$ and
invariance of the target gives $f(g\cdot x)=f(x)$, so $|q(g\cdot x)-f(x)|<\varepsilon$ for each
$g\in\Gamma$; averaging and using the triangle inequality preserves the strict bound.
\end{proof}

\section{The positive half: symmetric universality}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric Universality]\label{thm:pos}
Let $(\Gamma,\act)$ be a finite token-symmetry group, $K\subseteq\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ compact and
$\Gamma$-saturated, $g$ a continuous $\Gamma$-invariant functional, and $\varepsilon>0$. Then
there exists $p\in\A$ that is $\Gamma$-invariant and satisfies $|p(x)-g(x)|<\varepsilon$ for all
$x\in K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sep}, $\A$ is a point-separating subalgebra containing the constants. The
Stone--Weierstrass theorem on the compact set $K$ yields $q\in\A$ with
$\sup_{x\in K}|q(x)-g(x)|<\varepsilon$. Put $p = \Sy_\Gamma q$ and apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:sym}(i)--(iii).
\end{proof}

The unconstrained approximant $q$ is generically not symmetric; symmetry is imposed afterwards,
by a projection, at zero cost in accuracy. Symmetry is not a constraint that must be respected
during the search --- it is a retraction applicable at the end.

\begin{corollary}[Permutation-symmetric transformers]\label{cor:perm}
Taking $\Gamma = \Sym(\iota)$: on every compact permutation-saturated domain, every continuous
permutation-invariant functional is uniformly approximated by permutation-invariant elements of
the attention algebra. This is exactly the reach of a positional-encoding-free attention stack.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Relative/shift symmetry]
Let $r\in\Sym(\iota)$, suppose $g(r^k\cdot x)=g(x)$ for all $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and that $K$
satisfies $x\in K\Rightarrow r^k\cdot x\in K$. Then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $p\in\A$
with $p(r^k\cdot x)=p(x)$ for all $k,x$ and $|p-g|<\varepsilon$ on $K$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} with $\Gamma = \langle r\rangle$, finite because $\Sym(\iota)$ is.
In a finite group the subgroup generated by $r$ coincides with the set of natural-number powers
of $r$, so the hypotheses transfer and the conclusion specializes back.
\end{proof}

\section{The negative half: a sharp barrier}

\begin{lemma}[Invariance defect inequality]\label{lem:defect}
Let $\sigma$ be a permutation, let $p$ satisfy $p(\sigma\cdot x)=p(x)$ for all $x$, and let $g$
be arbitrary. Then for all $x$,
\[
  \bigl| g(x)-g(\sigma\cdot x) \bigr| \;\le\; \bigl| p(x)-g(x)\bigr|
  + \bigl| p(\sigma\cdot x)-g(\sigma\cdot x)\bigr|.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Using $p(\sigma\cdot x)=p(x)$ we have $g(x)-g(\sigma\cdot x) = -(p(x)-g(x)) +
(p(\sigma\cdot x)-g(\sigma\cdot x))$; apply the triangle inequality.
\end{proof}

The left-hand side depends on the target alone; the right-hand side is at most twice the
model's uniform error. Everything below follows.

\begin{theorem}[The symmetric class is uniformly closed]\label{thm:closed}
Let $\sigma$ be a permutation and $K$ a $\sigma$-stable set. If for every $\varepsilon>0$ there
is a $\sigma$-invariant $p$ with $|p-g|<\varepsilon$ on $K$, then $g(\sigma\cdot x)=g(x)$ for
every $x\in K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\delta := |g(x)-g(\sigma\cdot x)| > 0$ for some $x\in K$. Choose $p$ $\sigma$-invariant
with uniform error $<\delta/4$ on $K$. Both $x$ and $\sigma\cdot x$ lie in $K$, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:defect} gives $\delta < \delta/2$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:pos}, the uniform closure of $\A^\Gamma$ on a compact saturated
$K$ is exactly the $\Gamma$-invariant continuous functions on $K$. The next result upgrades
closedness from qualitative to quantitative, and it is sharp.

\begin{theorem}[Orbit Barrier, lower bound]\label{thm:orbitlow}
Let $g:\Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\R$, let $x$ be a sequence, and set
\[
  \osc(g,x) \;=\; \max_{\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)} g(\sigma\cdot x)
  \;-\; \min_{\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)} g(\sigma\cdot x).
\]
Then every permutation-invariant $p$ satisfies
$\max_{\sigma} \bigl| p(\sigma\cdot x)-g(\sigma\cdot x)\bigr| \ge \osc(g,x)/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\sigma_M,\sigma_m$ attain the maximum and minimum of $\sigma\mapsto g(\sigma\cdot x)$ over
the finite group $\Sym(\iota)$, and let $E$ denote the left-hand side. Invariance gives
$p(\sigma_M\cdot x) = p(x) = p(\sigma_m\cdot x) =: c$. Then $g(\sigma_M\cdot x) - c \le E$ and
$c - g(\sigma_m\cdot x) \le E$; adding gives $\osc(g,x)\le 2E$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit Barrier, attained]\label{thm:orbithigh}
With $M = \max_\sigma g(\sigma\cdot x)$ and $m = \min_\sigma g(\sigma\cdot x)$, the constant
model $p\equiv (M+m)/2$ is permutation-invariant and satisfies
$\bigl| p(\sigma\cdot x)-g(\sigma\cdot x)\bigr| \le \osc(g,x)/2$ for every $\sigma$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A constant is invariant under everything, and $m\le g(\sigma\cdot x)\le M$ gives
$-\tfrac{M-m}{2}\le \tfrac{M+m}{2}-g(\sigma\cdot x)\le \tfrac{M-m}{2}$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:orbitlow} and~\ref{thm:orbithigh} together give an \emph{equality}: the
optimal invariant approximation error on a single orbit is exactly $\osc(g,x)/2$, achieved by
the orbit midpoint. This is a barrier with no slack.

\begin{theorem}[Position-reading barrier]\label{thm:posread}
Let $\sigma$ be a token symmetry with $\sigma(i_0)\neq i_0$, fix a feature $a_0$, and let $p$ be
any $\sigma$-invariant functional. Then there is a sequence $x$ with
$\bigl| p(x)-x_{i_0,a_0}\bigr| \ge \tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $u_{i,a} = \one_{\{i=i_0,\,a=a_0\}}$, so $u_{i_0,a_0}=1$ while
$(\sigma\cdot u)_{i_0,a_0} = u_{\sigma(i_0),a_0}=0$. Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:defect} to the
target $g(y)=y_{i_0,a_0}$ at $u$ gives $1 \le |p(u)-1| + |p(\sigma\cdot u)-0|$. If
$|p(u)-1|\ge 1/2$ take $x=u$; otherwise the inequality forces
$|p(\sigma\cdot u)-(\sigma\cdot u)_{i_0,a_0}| > 1/2$ and we take $x=\sigma\cdot u$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:posread} applies verbatim to fully permutation-symmetric models and to
shift-symmetric ones: relative positional encodings do not read absolute position, and the
failure is at least $1/2$ in the sup norm on the unit cube.

\begin{corollary}
No permutation-invariant functional $p$ of two-token, one-feature sequences approximates ``the
first coordinate'' to uniform accuracy better than $1/2$: there is always an $x\in\R^2$ with
$|p(x)-x_1|\ge 1/2$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The repair: positional encoding restores universality}

\begin{definition}[Invariant decoder]
$\dec : \Seq(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\iota)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ is
\[
  \dec(z)_{i,a} \;=\; \sum_{j\in\iota} z_{j,\,\pos(i)}\; z_{j,\,\feat(a)},
\]
where $\pos(i)$ is coordinate $i$ of the positional block and $\feat(a)$ is coordinate $a$ of
the feature block. This is exactly one attention pattern: the positional block supplies the
weights, the feature block supplies the values.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dec}
$\dec$ is continuous, inverts the encoding ($\dec(\enc(x))=x$), and is permutation-invariant
($\dec(\sigma\cdot z)=\dec(z)$ for every $\sigma$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Continuity: each output coordinate is a finite sum of products of projections. Inversion:
$\enc(x)_{j,\pos(i)} = \one_{\{i=j\}}$, so the sum collapses to $j=i$, leaving
$\enc(x)_{i,\feat(a)} = x_{i,a}$. Invariance: replacing $z$ by $\sigma\cdot z$ reindexes the
sum by the bijection $\sigma$, which does not change a finite sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Encoded saturation]
$\widehat{K} = \bigcup_{\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)} \sigma\cdot(\enc(K))$. It contains $\enc(K)$, is
permutation-saturated by construction, and is compact whenever $K$ is, being a finite union of
continuous images of a compact set.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Positional encoding restores universality]\label{thm:posuniv}
Let $g$ be \emph{any} continuous functional of sequences, $K$ compact (no saturation
hypothesis), $\varepsilon>0$. Then there is a permutation-invariant
$p\in\A(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\iota)$ with $\bigl| p(\enc(x))-g(x)\bigr| < \varepsilon$ for all
$x\in K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $G = g\circ\dec$, continuous and permutation-invariant by Lemma~\ref{lem:dec}. Apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:perm} on the compact saturated $\widehat{K}$ to get a permutation-invariant
$p\in\A(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\iota)$ with $|p-G|<\varepsilon$ on $\widehat{K}$. For $x\in K$ we
have $\enc(x)\in\widehat{K}$ and $G(\enc(x)) = g(\dec(\enc(x))) = g(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sequence-to-sequence universality with positional encoding]
Let $F:\Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ have all coordinates $x\mapsto F(x)_{i,a}$
continuous, let $K$ be compact and $\varepsilon>0$. Then there is a family $(P_{i,a})$ of
permutation-invariant elements of $\A(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\iota)$ with
$\bigl| P_{i,a}(\enc(x))-F(x)_{i,a}\bigr| < \varepsilon$ for all $x\in K$ and all $i,a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:posuniv} to each of the finitely many coordinate functionals with the
same $K$ and $\varepsilon$, and collect the models. Finiteness of $\iota$ and $\kappa$ makes the
accuracy simultaneous over all output slots at no loss.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Positional encoding is \emph{not} symmetry breaking. The approximating models remain exactly
permutation-invariant as functions on the augmented space; what changed is that the input now
carries its own order, in a form a symmetric model may consult. The decoder is the explicit
witness: an invariant, attention-shaped operation reconstructing the ordered sequence from its
tagged version. Note also that $\dec$ is quadratic in its input --- the same bilinear
``weights times values'' shape as an attention head --- so the repair is implementable by the
very mechanism it repairs.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact converse: equivariant sequence-to-sequence maps}

\begin{lemma}[Marking intertwines the action]\label{lem:mark}
For all $\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)$, $i$, $x$: $m_i(\sigma\cdot x) = \sigma\cdot m_{\sigma(i)}(x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Compare token $j$. On the feature block both sides equal $x_{\sigma(j)}$. On the mark
coordinate the left side is $\one_{\{j=i\}}$ and the right is $\one_{\{\sigma(j)=\sigma(i)\}}$;
these agree by injectivity of $\sigma$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:head}
If $G$ is $\sigma$-invariant then the head $i\mapsto G(m_i(x))$ is $\sigma$-equivariant:
$G(m_i(\sigma\cdot x)) = G(m_{\sigma(i)}(x))$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Marked functional]
For $F:\Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ and $a\in\kappa$, define on marked sequences
\[
  G^F_a(z) \;=\; \sum_{i\in\iota} F\bigl(\feat(z)\bigr)_{i,a}\; \mrk(z)_i .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:markfun}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item If all coordinates of $F$ are continuous then each $G^F_a$ is continuous.
\item If $F$ is $\Gamma$-equivariant then each $G^F_a$ is $\Gamma$-invariant.
\item $G^F_a(m_i(x)) = F(x)_{i,a}$ for all $x,i,a$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(i) $\feat$ and $\mrk$ are projections, and a finite sum of products of continuous functions is
continuous. (ii) Since $\feat(g\cdot z)=g\cdot\feat(z)$ and $\mrk(g\cdot z)_i =
\mrk(z)_{\act(g)(i)}$, equivariance gives
\[
  G^F_a(g\cdot z) = \sum_i F(\feat(z))_{\act(g)(i),a}\,\mrk(z)_{\act(g)(i)} = G^F_a(z),
\]
the last step by reindexing along the bijection $\act(g)$. (iii) $\feat(m_i(x))=x$ and
$\mrk(m_i(x))_{i'} = \one_{\{i'=i\}}$, so the sum collapses.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equivariance--Invariance Representation Theorem]\label{thm:rep}
For $F:\Seq(\iota,\kappa)\to\Seq(\iota,\kappa)$ the following are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item All coordinates of $F$ are continuous and $F$ is $\Gamma$-equivariant.
\item There is a family $(G_a)_{a\in\kappa}$ of continuous $\Gamma$-\emph{invariant} functionals
on marked sequences with $F(x)_{i,a} = G_a(m_i(x))$ for all $x,i,a$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$: take $G_a = G^F_a$ and invoke Lemma~\ref{lem:markfun}.
$(2)\Rightarrow(1)$: each coordinate is the composite of the continuous $m_i$ with the
continuous $G_a$; and $F(g\cdot x)_{i,a} = G_a(m_i(g\cdot x)) = G_a(m_{\act(g)(i)}(x)) =
F(x)_{\act(g)(i),a}$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:head}.
\end{proof}

Equivariant maps and invariant functionals are the same data, read through the marked-token
window. With this, equivariant universality is a corollary of the invariant case.

\begin{theorem}[Equivariant Universality]\label{thm:equiv}
Let $(\Gamma,\act)$ be a finite token-symmetry group, $F$ a continuous $\Gamma$-equivariant
sequence-to-sequence map, $K$ compact, $\varepsilon>0$. Then there is a family $(P_a)_{a\in\kappa}$
with:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $P_a\in\A(\iota,\kappa\sqcup\{\ast\})$ for every $a$;
\item each $P_a$ is $\Gamma$-invariant;
\item the induced head family is \emph{exactly} $\Gamma$-equivariant:
$P_a(m_i(g\cdot x)) = P_a(m_{\act(g)(i)}(x))$ for all $a,g,i,x$;
\item $\bigl| P_a(m_i(x)) - F(x)_{i,a}\bigr| < \varepsilon$ for all $x\in K$ and all $i,a$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $a$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:markfun} the marked functional $G^F_a$ is continuous and
$\Gamma$-invariant. Let $\widetilde{K} = \bigcup_{g\in\Gamma}\bigcup_{i\in\iota} g\cdot(m_i(K))$,
a finite union of continuous images of a compact set, hence compact, and $\Gamma$-saturated
because applying $\tau$ to $g\cdot m_i(K)$ lands in $(g\tau)\cdot m_i(K)$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} supplies a $\Gamma$-invariant $P_a\in\A$ with $|P_a-G^F_a|<\varepsilon$ on
$\widetilde{K}$. Claim (3) is Corollary~\ref{cor:head}; claim (4) follows since $m_i(x)\in
\widetilde{K}$ for $x\in K$ and $G^F_a(m_i(x)) = F(x)_{i,a}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Full permutation equivariance]
With $\Gamma=\Sym(\iota)$: positional-encoding-free attention, queried through the marked-token
encoding, is a universal approximator of the continuous permutation-equivariant
sequence-to-sequence maps, and the approximants are exactly equivariant.
\end{corollary}

Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:posread}, which shows that a non-equivariant target is unreachable
by a margin of at least $1/2$, this pins down the expressive power of symmetric attention
exactly: it is the class of continuous equivariant maps, nothing less and nothing more. Point
(3) of Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} matters for applications: the approximation error appears only in
the values, while the symmetry identity holds on the nose, at every input, for every model in
the constructed family.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Group symmetrization.} Given a model $q$ and a symmetry group $\Gamma$, return
$\frac{1}{|\Gamma|}\sum_{g\in\Gamma} q\circ(\act(g)\cdot(-))$. Cost: $|\Gamma|$ evaluations per
forward pass; for readouts, the permuted model is obtained simply by permuting the rows of the
weight table, at no evaluation cost. Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} guarantees each summand is again in
the architecture class.

\paragraph{Exact orbit barrier.} Given a target $g$, a base sequence $x$, and a group $\Gamma$,
compute $M$ and $m$, the maximum and minimum of $g$ over the orbit; return optimal error
$(M-m)/2$ and optimal constant $(M+m)/2$. Correctness is
Theorems~\ref{thm:orbitlow}--\ref{thm:orbithigh}; cost is $|\Gamma|$ target evaluations. For the
``read position $i_0$'' target on the unit cube the output is exactly $1/2$, recovering
Theorem~\ref{thm:posread} numerically.

\paragraph{Positional decode-and-approximate.} Given $g$, a compact $K$ and $\varepsilon$: form
$G = g\circ\dec$ on the augmented space, fit a polynomial in readouts to $G$ on $\widehat{K}$
within $\varepsilon$, then symmetrize. The decode step is itself a single quadratic attention
pattern, so the whole pipeline is expressible in the architecture.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\emph{Positional encodings, explained rather than assumed.} The folklore ``transformers need
positional encodings'' acquires a precise form: without them a model is confined to
permutation-invariant functionals (Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}); the confinement costs exactly half
the orbit oscillation (Theorems~\ref{thm:orbitlow}--\ref{thm:orbithigh}); reading absolute
position costs at least $1/2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:posread}); and one-hot tags restore everything
(Theorem~\ref{thm:posuniv}) without breaking symmetry.

\emph{Relative encodings are a genuinely weaker regime.} Theorem~\ref{thm:posread} needs only
\emph{some} symmetry moving $i_0$, so a shift-symmetric model inherits the same $1/2$ barrier
for absolute-position targets, while remaining fully expressive for shift-invariant structure.

\emph{Set and graph models.} Architectures for unordered data impose permutation symmetry
deliberately; Corollary~\ref{cor:perm} says they are fully expressive for the invariant targets
they are designed for, and Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} says their per-element predictions are fully
expressive too, with exact structural symmetry.

\emph{Data augmentation versus architectural symmetry.} Lemma~\ref{lem:sym}(iii) says
symmetrizing never degrades accuracy on a symmetric target, giving a theoretical underpinning
for averaging predictions over the group at test time.

\emph{A design criterion.} Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} identifies the one property an architecture must
have for the theory to apply: closure under relabelling of positions. A proposed layer failing
it cannot be symmetrized, and none of the positive results survive.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Head-count law]
For every $\Gamma$-invariant continuous $g$ on a compact saturated $K$ and every
$\varepsilon>0$ there is a $\Gamma$-invariant element of $\A$ that $\varepsilon$-approximates
$g$ and is a sum of at most $\mathrm{poly}(d,1/\varepsilon)$ products of readouts --- in
particular a bound independent of $|\Gamma|$.
\end{conjecture}

The intuition is that averaging is a projection, not a construction: the invariant subalgebra is
already generated by the multisymmetric power sums $x\mapsto \sum_i\prod_a (x_{i,a})^{e_a}$, each
a single product of readouts. Eliminating the group sum in favour of such a generating set would
turn an exponential construction into a polynomial one. The conjecture is falsifiable by finite
computation at small $n$, tested against the $1/2$ barrier.

\begin{conjecture}[Softmax realizability]
The uniform closure on compacta of finite compositions of genuine softmax attention layers with
residual connections and coordinatewise multiplicative gating contains the whole attention
algebra $\A$, with explicit depth and width bounds.
\end{conjecture}

Proposition~\ref{prop:head} gives the base case; the content is that products of readouts are
realizable by composition and gating rather than merely postulated.

\begin{conjecture}[Approximate symmetry]
If a model is only $\eta$-approximately invariant, its optimal error on an orbit is at least
$\osc(g,x)/2 - O(\eta)$, giving a continuous trade-off between symmetry and expressivity and a
principled way to price ``soft'' positional encodings.
\end{conjecture}

Two further programmes. First, \emph{infinite and variable-length index sets}: extending to
length-generalizing models, where $\iota$ varies and uniformity in $n$ is demanded, requires
replacing $\Sym(\iota)$ by a compact group with Haar measure. The averaging argument survives
verbatim with Haar averaging; the open question is whether approximants can be chosen with
$n$-independent complexity. Second, \emph{optimal encodings}: the one-hot encoding uses $n$
extra features per token. We conjecture a lower bound of $\Omega(\log n)$ extra features for any
continuous encoding admitting an invariant continuous decoder, and ask whether $O(\log n)$
suffices --- which would explain the empirical success of low-dimensional sinusoidal encodings.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, two-sided description of the expressive power of attention
architectures constrained by a finite group of token symmetries. Symmetric attention is
uniformly dense in the symmetric continuous functionals on compact saturated domains, for any
finite symmetry group; the proof is Stone--Weierstrass followed by free group averaging,
legitimate because the attention algebra is stable under relabelling positions. Conversely the
symmetric class is uniformly closed, and the optimal error outside it is exactly half the orbit
oscillation --- in particular, reading an absolute position costs at least $1/2$. One-hot
positional encoding removes the deficit entirely, via an explicitly invariant attention-shaped
decoder, with no loss of symmetry in the model. In the sequence-to-sequence setting,
equivariance and invariance are the same data through the marked-token window, so symmetric
attention approximates exactly the continuous equivariant maps, with symmetry holding on the
nose. What is most satisfying is the absence of a gap: density and closedness meet; the lower
bound and its achievability meet. A single number --- half the orbit oscillation --- says
everything about what symmetry costs, and a single construction --- tag the positions, decode
invariantly --- says everything about how to get it back.

